# 1-based lines to bookmark. Each sits INSIDE a construct the profile claims to
# understand, so a broken `containers` shows up as a missing breadcrumb.
#
# Swift has no namespace construct, so a type is the outermost level. What this
# file asserts instead is that every *kind* of type declaration works as a
# container — class, struct, enum, protocol, extension — and that initialisers
# and computed properties behave as the profile claims.
#
# Switch arms are given different shapes on purpose: identical sibling
# statements make the generated structural query ambiguous and codemark refuses
# the bookmark.

# Inside the computed property on an enum — proves `enum` is a container,
# which works only because the enum holds a declaration and not just cases.
34

# Inside the subscript — a declaration with no identifier name at all, which
# the concept schema handles because a container needs a body, not a name.
102

# Inside the computed property's explicit getter body.
109

# Inside `deinit` — likewise nameless.
97

# Inside a switch arm inside the for loop in compute(). The deepest chain here:
# class > method > for > switch > arm.
124

# Inside the local `func rounded` declared in compute()'s body — a declaration
# nested in a function, which only idiomatic code produces.
135

# Inside the async method's trailing-closure chain.
147

# Inside render() on the nested `struct Receipt` — proves type-in-type
# containment: class > struct > func.
176

# Inside pay() on `extension Invoice: Payable` — an extension is a container
# kind of its own, and sits outside the class body entirely.
189

# Inside the default implementation on a protocol extension.
197

# NOT bookmarkable, recorded rather than silently omitted:
#
#   89    guard !lines.isEmpty else {
#   90        throw InvoiceError.noLines
#   93    self.lines = lines
#
# No line inside `init` can be bookmarked. An `init_declaration` has no name
# field, so for any node whose nearest landmark ancestor is the initialiser the
# generator emits an impossible `name:` pattern and tree-sitter rejects the
# query outright. Reproduces with an empty profile, so no curation or schema
# change fixes it — this is codemark#239. The subscript and the computed
# property above are the nameless declarations that *do* work, because their
# enclosing landmark is the class.
